The first paragraph introduces the core concept of weighted sampling for edge selection in graph G, which is central to addressing the problem of costly function space sampling within thin regions. It outlines a novel approach that involves using ranking functions and Chebyshev centers to guide the sampling process, thus contributing significantly to the scientific contribution of the paper segment by proposing an effective methodology for obtaining weights in edge sampling.

The second paragraph details the algorithms (SortedLB ES and MaxO) used to implement the weighted edge sampling approach described in Paragraph 1. It provides a step-by-step explanation of how these algorithms operate, including input parameters, outputs, and pseudocode for SortedLB ES. While this section is crucial for understanding the implementation details and practical application of the proposed methods, it builds upon the foundational concepts introduced in Paragraph 1 rather than introducing new core scientific contributions on its own.

Therefore, based on their respective contributions to the main scientific contribution of the parent segment, I have assigned a higher percentage weightage to Paragraph 1 and a lower one to Paragraph 2.
