In this task, we present a novel algorithm called Uniform Random Mutation (URM) for finding compact representatives from large datasets with many dimensions. We propose URM as an anytime algorithm that can be terminated at any point in time while still providing the user with a reasonably good representative of the data set. 

The HD-RRMS algorithm is a hill climbing approach to find a representative subset of tuples from a dataset, where each tuple represents a record and its score is computed based on a given decision criteria. The goal is to minimize this score (or regret ratio) while keeping the size of the output set small. 

The URM algorithm improves upon HD-RRMS by introducing randomness in the search process, which allows it to escape from local optima and potentially find a better representative even with multiple runs or restarts. The experiments show that URM quickly reaches the local minima of regret ratio in this setting. Also, in most of the experiments, URM improves the results of the HD-RRMS algorithm. 

The performance of the algorithm is evaluated on four datasets: Surface ,Colors ,NBA and DOT . The results show that with a fixed time budget, we can find reasonably good representatives even when the allocated time budget is small. This means that a user can still get a reasonably good set of compact representatives even if she terminates the algorithm before it finishes. 

